        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Airborne transmission occurs when infectious particles remain suspended and can be inhaled over time or distance under conditions that support airborne spread.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Small, respirable particles can follow air currents and reach the lower or upper respiratory tract. Ventilation, room pressure, filtration, respiratory protection, proximity, and exposure duration modify risk.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Tuberculosis, measles, and varicella are classic healthcare examples for which CDC recommends organism-specific Airborne Precautions.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Place a patient in the appropriate airborne-infection setting and use fit-tested respiratory protection when current guidance requires it; rapidly escalate isolation needs rather than relying on distance alone.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Aerosols can be generated in many situations, but aerosol generation by itself does not prove that a pathogen is predominantly airborne or establish the required isolation category.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC isolation guidance distinguishes airborne transmission and the engineering and respiratory-protection controls used for selected infections.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Airborne transmission occurs when infectious particles remain suspended and can be inhaled over time or distance under conditions that support airborne spread.",
