        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "An antimicrobial resistance phenotype is the observable pattern of susceptibility and resistance produced by an organism under standardized testing.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "The phenotype reflects the combined effect of expressed genes, mutations, permeability, efflux, growth state, and test conditions. It is the clinically measured consequence, not necessarily the name of a single mechanism.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, vancomycin-resistant enterococci, and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales are clinically important phenotype labels with distinct definitions and local implications.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use the current isolate's susceptibility report and the laboratory's interpretive standards. Communicate important resistant organisms promptly because therapy and infection-control decisions may both change.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Resistance labels are not interchangeable, and breakpoints or definitions may change. A class label never substitutes for the actual organism, specimen, and susceptibility results.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "WHO and CDC guidance links reliable susceptibility interpretation with surveillance, infection prevention, and appropriate antimicrobial use.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
