        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "A biofilm is an organized microbial community attached to a surface and embedded in a self-produced matrix.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "The matrix promotes adherence, limits penetration of some host factors and antimicrobials, supports metabolic diversity, and facilitates communication and gene exchange. Slow-growing or protected cells may persist even when free-floating organisms are more susceptible.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Biofilms are important on teeth, chronic wounds, airway mucus, catheters, prosthetic joints, heart valves, and other implanted material.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Biofilm-associated infection can relapse unless the source is controlled. Management may require drainage, debridement, or device evaluation in addition to an active antimicrobial chosen from organism and susceptibility data.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Not every persistent infection is a biofilm infection, and an in-vitro biofilm observation does not automatically determine bedside treatment.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI Medical Microbiology describes adherence and surface-associated microbial communities as clinically important contributors to persistence.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
