        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Bloodborne transmission occurs when infectious blood enters another person's bloodstream or contacts susceptible mucosa or nonintact skin.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Percutaneous sharps injuries, unsafe injections, transfusion, shared injection equipment, and selected mucosal or nonintact-skin exposures can transfer a sufficient inoculum; risk depends on agent, source burden, route, volume, and host immunity.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Hepatitis B, hepatitis C, and HIV are major occupational bloodborne concerns, but their transmission probabilities and post-exposure options differ.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Immediately perform first aid, report the exposure, document the route and source, and obtain time-sensitive occupational evaluation rather than estimating risk from visible blood alone.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Intact-skin contact is not equivalent to a percutaneous exposure, and not every body fluid carries the same bloodborne risk.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC/NIOSH describes bloodborne occupational routes and the factors that determine exposure risk; Standard Precautions reduce preventable contact.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
