        "name": "Body-fluid transmission",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "body fluid exposure transmission",
          "Body-fluid transmission",
          "infectious body fluids",
          "mucosal body fluid exposure"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Body-fluid transmission is spread through a specific infectious secretion or excretion contacting a susceptible portal of entry.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Saliva, urine, genital secretions, breast milk, blood, respiratory secretions, or other fluids can carry different agents; transmission requires that the relevant fluid reach compatible mucosa, damaged skin, tissue, a device, or the bloodstream.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "CMV can spread through saliva, urine, blood, semen, and breast milk, whereas many other agents are restricted to a narrower set of fluids and routes.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Identify the exact fluid, organism, route, timing, barrier integrity, and exposed person before choosing testing, prophylaxis, or counseling.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "The phrase 'body fluids' is too broad for risk prediction. Sweat, intact-skin contact, saliva, blood, and genital secretions are not interchangeable exposures.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC core infection-control practices address exposure to blood and body substances and require route-specific assessment rather than a generic fluid label.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
