        "name": "Clostridioides difficile toxin A",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "C diff enterotoxin",
          "C difficile toxin A",
          "Clostridioides difficile toxin A",
          "TcdA",
          "toxin A CDI"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Clostridioides difficile toxin A, abbreviated TcdA, is one of the major glucosylating toxins produced by toxigenic C. difficile strains and contributes to intestinal epithelial injury and inflammation.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "After entering susceptible intestinal cells, TcdA modifies Rho-family regulatory proteins. Cytoskeletal signaling and tight-junction integrity fail, epithelial permeability rises, and inflammatory and secretory responses promote diarrhea and colitis. The mechanism connects a molecular toxin effect to fluid loss and mucosal damage.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "TcdA is often taught with TcdB, but they are distinct toxins and not interchangeable names. A strain's toxin profile must be demonstrated by appropriate microbiological methods rather than assumed from the species name.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "TcdA contributes to the pathophysiology of symptomatic CDI. The clinically important question remains whether toxin-mediated disease is occurring in a compatible patient, not merely whether C. difficile DNA or colonization is present.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "TcdA is not required as the sole toxin in every clinically significant strain; TcdA-negative and TcdB-positive disease exists. A toxin result must be interpreted within the assay and diagnostic algorithm, and this concept card does not replace the C. difficile diagnostic-test card.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed against CDC clinical guidance and peer-reviewed experimental work differentiating TcdA and TcdB.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Clostridioides difficile toxin A, abbreviated TcdA, is one of the major glucosylating toxins produced by toxigenic C. difficile strains and contributes to intestinal epithelial injury and inflammation.",
        "type": "microbiology",
