        "name": "Droplet transmission",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "close-range respiratory spread",
          "droplet precautions",
          "Droplet transmission",
          "respiratory droplet spread"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Droplet transmission is close-range spread when respiratory particles deposit directly on susceptible mucous membranes or are transferred during near-patient contact.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Coughing, sneezing, speaking, and procedures release particles that can reach the eyes, nose, or mouth before dilution, settling, or removal. Proximity and source control therefore strongly influence exposure.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pertussis, meningococcal disease, and seasonal influenza are common examples in which current guidance may require Droplet Precautions.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Combine the indicated mask and eye protection with source control, hand hygiene, placement, and organism-specific duration of precautions.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Droplet and airborne behavior overlap physically. The clinical category is a prevention framework, not proof that only one particle size or one route is involved.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC Appendix A and the scientific review connect specific infections with Droplet Precautions while acknowledging multiple transmission contributors.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Droplet transmission is close-range spread when respiratory particles deposit directly on susceptible mucous membranes or are transferred during near-patient contact.",
