        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Fecal-oral transmission occurs when an infectious agent shed in feces reaches another person's mouth, usually through contaminated hands, food, water, equipment, or environmental surfaces.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "The route links intestinal shedding to ingestion. Poor hand hygiene, unsafe sanitation, cross-contamination during food preparation, and environmentally persistent forms such as spores can keep that chain intact. Hand hygiene, safe water, food controls, toilet sanitation, and environmental cleaning interrupt different steps.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Enteric pathogenic E. coli may spread in contaminated food or water, while C. difficile spores may move from fecally contaminated healthcare surfaces or hands. These examples share a route but differ in ecology, persistence, testing, and prevention details.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Recognizing the route helps clinicians choose exposure questions, specimen strategies, patient education, outbreak controls, and precautions. It also explains why protecting only the symptomatic patient is insufficient when contaminated food, water, or shared environments remain part of the chain.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Fecal-oral is a transmission route, not proof that every intestinal organism is pathogenic. Normal gut organisms can be present without disease, and some species also cause endogenous extraintestinal infection through mechanisms that are not person-to-person fecal-oral spread.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed against CDC E. coli, C. difficile, isolation-precautions, and infection-prevention guidance.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
