        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Microbial genetics describes inherited and acquired changes in microbial genetic material. Horizontal gene transfer moves genetic information between organisms rather than only from parent to offspring.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Mutation, recombination, plasmids, bacteriophages, and mobile genetic elements can change virulence, antigen expression, and drug susceptibility. Selection favors variants that survive a particular immune or antimicrobial pressure.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacteria may acquire genes through transformation, transduction, or conjugation. Viruses may accumulate mutations or exchange genome segments, and resistance determinants can spread across related or sometimes unrelated bacteria.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "The concept explains why resistance can emerge during therapy, why local susceptibility patterns change, and why genomic epidemiology can link outbreaks. Stewardship and infection prevention reduce opportunities for selection and spread.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Detection of a gene does not always prove that it is expressed or clinically dominant. Phenotypic susceptibility and patient context remain important.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI Medical Microbiology and WHO antimicrobial-resistance guidance describe genetic change, selection, and spread as central mechanisms of microbial adaptation.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Microbial genetics describes inherited and acquired changes in microbial genetic material. Horizontal gene transfer moves genetic information between organisms rather than only from parent to offspring.",
