        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Microbial pathogenesis is the sequence by which an organism reaches a host, establishes itself, evades or exploits defenses, damages tissue, and produces clinical disease.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Disease can result from direct invasion, toxin effects, immune-mediated injury, vascular disruption, nutrient competition, inflammation, or combinations of these processes. The same organism may cause colonization in one person and invasive disease in another because host barriers and immunity differ.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Adherence, invasion, intracellular survival, capsule-mediated immune evasion, toxin production, and biofilm formation are common pathogenic strategies.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Understanding the mechanism connects symptoms, laboratory findings, complications, diagnostic timing, and treatment priorities instead of treating them as isolated facts.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Association does not prove causation, and disease severity reflects both microbial properties and host response. Mechanisms should be stated at the organism or syndrome level when evidence supports them.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI Medical Microbiology presents host entry, microbial multiplication, immune interaction, and tissue injury as connected stages of pathogenesis.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
