        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Microbial structure and morphology describe an infectious agent's physical organization, shape, surface features, and major cellular or acellular components. These features help clinicians organize a differential diagnosis, understand how an agent interacts with the host, and choose appropriate diagnostic methods.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Structure matters because envelopes, cell walls, capsules, spores, membranes, and surface proteins affect environmental survival, immune recognition, staining behavior, and antimicrobial susceptibility. Morphology is therefore a clue rather than an organism-level diagnosis.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clinically useful patterns include cocci, bacilli, curved or spiral bacteria, yeasts, molds, enveloped and non-enveloped viruses, protozoan life stages, helminth eggs or larvae, and abnormal prion protein.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pair morphology with specimen source, host factors, exposure history, and confirmatory testing. A shape or stain pattern can narrow urgent empiric decisions, but final interpretation depends on the complete clinical context.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Different organisms may share a morphology, and one organism may change appearance with growth phase or specimen conditions. Never equate a visual pattern alone with definitive identification.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI Medical Microbiology explains how microbial form and structure support classification, pathogenesis, and laboratory recognition.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
