        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Sexual transmission is passage of an infectious agent during oral, anal, or vaginal sex or related genital skin-to-skin contact.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Mucosal microtrauma, genital secretions, blood, semen, vaginal fluid, and direct contact with infectious skin or lesions provide agent-specific portals of entry.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Gonorrhea and chlamydia commonly involve infected secretions and mucosa, while syphilis, herpes, and HPV can spread through contact with infectious lesions or skin not fully covered by a barrier.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Testing sites, partner services, pregnancy assessment, vaccination where applicable, and prevention counseling should match the exposure and organism.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "A sexually associated infection is not proof of a person's behavior, timing, source, consent, or relationship history; avoid stigmatizing inference.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC STI guidance explains the different sexual contact routes and the role of testing, treatment, vaccination, and partner prevention.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
