        "name": "Staphylococcal protein A",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "IgG Fc binding protein",
          "S aureus protein A",
          "SpA",
          "staphylococcal immune evasion",
          "Staphylococcal protein A"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Staphylococcal protein A is a cell-wall-associated and secreted virulence factor of Staphylococcus aureus that binds immunoglobulins and alters effective antibody-mediated host defense.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Protein A binds the Fc region of immunoglobulin G. Normally, Fc regions point outward after antibodies bind a microbe so phagocytes can recognize and ingest the coated organism. Protein A disrupts that useful orientation and weakens opsonization and phagocytic clearance; interactions with B-cell receptors can also distort adaptive immune responses.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Its classic exam association is Fc binding by S. aureus. That association explains immune evasion, but it should be integrated with adhesins, toxins, enzymes, biofilm, host barriers, and resistance rather than memorized as the organism's only pathogenic tool.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Protein A helps explain how S. aureus can persist despite an antibody response and why colonization can progress to invasive disease when barriers fail. It is a mechanism concept, not a standalone diagnostic result or a medication target in routine bedside care.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Virulence depends on strain expression, infection site, inoculum, and host defenses. Detecting or recalling protein A does not determine methicillin susceptibility, prove invasive infection, or replace culture and susceptibility testing.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed against NCBI Medical Microbiology and peer-reviewed experimental work on protein A-mediated immune evasion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Staphylococcal protein A is a cell-wall-associated and secreted virulence factor of Staphylococcus aureus that binds immunoglobulins and alters effective antibody-mediated host defense.",
