        "name": "Vector-borne transmission",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "arthropod-borne transmission",
          "flea transmission",
          "mosquito transmission",
          "tick transmission",
          "Vector-borne transmission"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Vector-borne transmission occurs when a living carrier, commonly a mosquito, tick, flea, or other arthropod, transfers an infectious agent between hosts.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "The agent may simply be carried by the vector or may develop or multiply within it. Vector species, feeding behavior, climate, geography, season, and reservoir hosts shape who is exposed.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Mosquitoes transmit agents such as dengue and malaria parasites; ticks transmit agents such as Borrelia burgdorferi and Anaplasma phagocytophilum.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ask about geography, season, outdoor activity, animal exposure, bite timing, prophylaxis, and symptom onset; use agent-specific testing rather than a generic 'bug bite' panel.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "A bite is often unnoticed, and finding a vector does not prove transmission. Different vectors in the same region carry different agents.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC vector-borne guidance explains that vectors carry pathogens among people, animals, and environments and that ecology shapes risk.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Vector-borne transmission occurs when a living carrier, commonly a mosquito, tick, flea, or other arthropod, transfers an infectious agent between hosts.",
