        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Waterborne transmission is exposure to an infectious agent through contaminated drinking, recreational, environmental, or healthcare water.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "People may ingest water, inhale aerosols, aspirate it, expose eyes or damaged skin, or acquire organisms from moist devices and surfaces. Water systems can act as both route and reservoir.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cryptosporidium may follow ingestion, Legionella follows inhalation or aspiration of contaminated aerosols, and Pseudomonas can exploit moist healthcare reservoirs.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Match the water source and portal of entry to the syndrome and host; protect vulnerable patients and escalate suspected facility clusters for environmental investigation.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "A water exposure is not a diagnosis, and routine environmental culturing without an epidemiologic question can be misleading.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC waterborne and healthcare-water guidance distinguishes ingestion, inhalation, aspiration, device, and environmental pathways.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Waterborne transmission is exposure to an infectious agent through contaminated drinking, recreational, environmental, or healthcare water.",
