        "name": "Zoonotic transmission",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "animal exposure infection",
          "animal-to-human transmission",
          "zoonosis",
          "Zoonotic transmission"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Zoonotic transmission is spread of an infectious agent between animals and people, directly or through food, water, vectors, or contaminated environments.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bites, scratches, respiratory material, feces, urine, tissues, animal products, vectors, and shared environments create distinct portals of entry.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Rabies can follow a mammalian bite, psittacosis follows exposure to infected birds, and plague can involve fleas, animals, or respiratory spread in a specific syndrome.",
            "id": "examples",
            "title": "Examples"
          },
          {
            "content": "Record the species, type of contact, geography, occupation, travel, vaccination, bite care, timing, and whether the animal is available for public-health assessment.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Animal contact is common and often unrelated to illness; the exposure must fit the agent's host range, route, and incubation.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC One Health guidance describes zoonoses as infections shared between animals and people through several distinct exposure pathways.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Zoonotic transmission is spread of an infectious agent between animals and people, directly or through food, water, vectors, or contaminated environments.",
