        "name": "Airborne precautions",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "AIIR",
          "airborne infection isolation",
          "Airborne isolation",
          "Airborne precautions",
          "N95 isolation",
          "negative pressure room",
          "TB precautions"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Airborne Precautions are transmission-based measures added to Standard Precautions for infectious agents that remain infectious while suspended in air and can be inhaled beyond immediate face-to-face droplet range.",
            "id": "scope",
            "title": "Scope"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use them for suspected or confirmed conditions listed in current infection-control guidance, including infectious pulmonary or laryngeal tuberculosis. The clinical syndrome can justify immediate precautions before laboratory confirmation because waiting may expose others.",
            "id": "indications",
            "title": "Indications"
          },
          {
            "content": "An airborne infection-isolation room controls airflow and exhaust, while a fit-tested respirator reduces inhalation of infectious particles. Source control on the patient during necessary transport reduces particles released into shared air. These layers interrupt different points in the same transmission pathway.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Promptly identify the risk, place the patient in an appropriate airborne infection-isolation room when available, keep the door managed under facility policy, require indicated fit-tested respiratory protection before entry, limit transport, apply patient source control during transport, and coordinate diagnostic and public-health steps without delaying emergency stabilization.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Procedure"
          },
          {
            "content": "A surgical mask on staff is not a substitute for a required respirator, and a negative early test does not always remove clinical suspicion. Room engineering, respirator programs, pathogen-specific duration, and contingency plans vary by facility and must follow current policy.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Never leave an unstable patient unattended while arranging isolation. Confirm respirator fit and medical clearance, protect communication and monitoring, explain the reason for precautions, and reassess discontinuation only against disease-specific criteria.",
            "id": "precautions",
            "title": "Precautions"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed against current CDC transmission-based and tuberculosis healthcare infection-control guidance.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
