        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Aseptic technique is the set of practices used to prevent unwanted microorganisms from entering a vulnerable site, sterile field, device, medication, or specimen.",
            "id": "scope",
            "title": "Scope"
          },
          {
            "content": "It is required whenever a procedure or task could introduce organisms into normally sterile tissue, a vascular or urinary device, a wound, a medication pathway, or a diagnostic specimen.",
            "id": "indications",
            "title": "Indications"
          },
          {
            "content": "Hand hygiene, appropriate barriers, field control, non-touch technique, correct skin antisepsis, and protection of sterile parts break transfer routes before contamination reaches the patient.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use the facility-approved technique matched to the procedure's risk. Prepare supplies first, identify sterile and nonsterile zones, replace any item whose sterility is uncertain, and stop when contamination occurs.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Procedure"
          },
          {
            "content": "Asepsis lowers risk but cannot compensate for an unnecessary device, prolonged exposure, poor source control, or failure to recognize infection.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Document breaches and corrective actions. Never label an uncertain field or item as sterile simply because contamination was not seen.",
            "id": "precautions",
            "title": "Precautions"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC Standard Precautions and isolation guidance place hand hygiene, barriers, injection safety, and contamination control at the center of patient protection.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
