        "name": "Environmental infection controls",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "air water surface infection control",
          "environmental cleaning",
          "Environmental infection controls",
          "healthcare environmental controls"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Environmental infection controls reduce exposure from surfaces, equipment, air, water, waste, laundry, construction, and shared care spaces.",
            "id": "scope",
            "title": "Scope"
          },
          {
            "content": "Controls intensify according to the organism, care setting, patient vulnerability, contamination burden, procedure, and outbreak status.",
            "id": "indications",
            "title": "Indications"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cleaning removes soil and organisms; disinfection inactivates susceptible organisms; engineering controls manage airflow, water, barriers, and traffic; workflow separates clean from contaminated tasks.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use a risk-based facility plan with assigned responsibility, approved products, monitored processes, appropriate room turnover, and escalation for spills or suspected environmental transmission.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Procedure"
          },
          {
            "content": "Visible cleanliness does not prove microbial safety, and indiscriminate chemical use can damage equipment or expose staff without improving control.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Match PPE, ventilation, product compatibility, and contact requirements to the task. Report clusters or environmental failures rather than repeatedly cleaning without investigation.",
            "id": "precautions",
            "title": "Precautions"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC isolation and disinfection guidance integrates environmental cleaning and engineering measures with hand hygiene and transmission-based precautions.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
