        "name": "Sterilization",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "critical medical devices",
          "medical device sterilization",
          "spore destruction",
          "sterile processing",
          "Sterilization"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Sterilization is a validated process that destroys or removes all forms of microbial life on an item, including bacterial spores.",
            "id": "scope",
            "title": "Scope"
          },
          {
            "content": "Critical devices that enter sterile tissue or the vascular system must be sterile because even a small surviving inoculum can cause invasive infection.",
            "id": "indications",
            "title": "Indications"
          },
          {
            "content": "Validated physical or chemical processes inactivate organisms through heat, moisture, oxidation, alkylation, radiation, filtration, or another approved mechanism matched to the device.",
            "id": "mechanism",
            "title": "Mechanism"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cleaning must occur before sterilization because soil can shield organisms. Trained sterile-processing personnel follow device instructions, load parameters, monitoring, packaging, storage, and traceability requirements.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Procedure"
          },
          {
            "content": "A cycle label alone does not prove successful processing. Wrong cleaning, packaging, load configuration, device compatibility, storage, or monitoring can invalidate sterility. Prions are a special exception: routine reprocessing may be inadequate for instruments exposed to high-infectivity tissue in suspected or confirmed CJD.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Do not improvise sterilization or reuse single-use products outside an approved reprocessing system. Quarantine a load when required indicators or records are unacceptable. For possible prion contamination, preserve instrument traceability and immediately follow current CJD-specific infection-control and manufacturer guidance.",
            "id": "precautions",
            "title": "Precautions"
          },
          {
            "content": "CDC guidance distinguishes sterilization from lower levels of decontamination, requires validated reprocessing for critical equipment, and identifies prion-contaminated high-risk instruments as needing specialized CJD procedures.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Sterilization is a validated process that destroys or removes all forms of microbial life on an item, including bacterial spores.",
        "type": "microbiology",
