        "name": "Blood agar medium",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "searchTerms": [
          "Blood agar medium",
          "blood agar plate",
          "enriched culture medium",
          "hemolysis on blood agar"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Blood agar is an enriched solid clinical culture medium that supports growth of many nonfastidious and some more demanding bacteria and permits observation of red-cell reactions around colonies.",
            "id": "definition-composition",
            "title": "Definition and composition"
          },
          {
            "content": "ANI records blood agar's primary schema class as enriched because it supports growth. It also has a differential readout: complete, partial, or absent visible hemolysis can contribute to a preliminary identification pattern.",
            "id": "purpose-selective-differential-basis",
            "title": "Purpose and selective or differential basis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clinical laboratories use validated, quality-controlled commercial or locally approved preparations. ANI does not provide a bench recipe or incubation protocol.",
            "id": "preparation-use",
            "title": "Preparation and use"
          },
          {
            "content": "Growth and hemolysis patterns are described together with colony morphology, Gram stain, specimen source, and additional identification tests.",
            "id": "expected-growth-appearance",
            "title": "Expected growth and appearance"
          },
          {
            "content": "A hemolysis pattern narrows possibilities but is not species-specific. Clinically meaningful interpretation requires an isolate identity and patient context.",
            "id": "interpretation",
            "title": "Interpretation"
          },
          {
            "content": "Prior therapy, mixed culture, atypical strains, and incubation conditions can alter appearance. Lack of growth does not exclude organisms requiring specialized methods.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cultured organisms can be more concentrated than the original specimen and must be handled only by trained personnel under appropriate laboratory controls.",
            "id": "safety-storage",
            "title": "Safety and storage"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI diagnostic principles describe enriched and differential media as tools within a larger identification workflow.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
