        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Biochemical identification distinguishes organisms by reproducible metabolic, enzymatic, or substrate-use patterns.",
            "id": "principle",
            "title": "Principle"
          },
          {
            "content": "It converts a set of observable reactions into an identification probability or defined organism group, often after culture yields an isolate.",
            "id": "purpose",
            "title": "Purpose"
          },
          {
            "content": "Testing is generally performed on a suitable laboratory isolate or validated specimen pathway, not by applying a generic reaction panel directly to every clinical sample.",
            "id": "specimen-preparation",
            "title": "Specimen and preparation"
          },
          {
            "content": "A laboratory uses validated commercial or reference systems with quality controls and interpretive databases. This encyclopedia describes clinical meaning rather than bench protocols.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Method"
          },
          {
            "content": "A coherent pattern supports identification when it matches expected morphology and source. Discordant reactions prompt repeat testing or an orthogonal method.",
            "id": "interpretation",
            "title": "Interpretation"
          },
          {
            "content": "Closely related organisms may share profiles, unusual strains may be misidentified, and database coverage differs. Molecular or mass-spectrometric confirmation may be needed.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Only trained laboratory staff should manipulate viable isolates under required containment.",
            "id": "safety",
            "title": "Safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI diagnostic principles describe phenotypic identification as one component of a layered laboratory diagnosis.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Biochemical identification distinguishes organisms by reproducible metabolic, enzymatic, or substrate-use patterns.",
