        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Nucleic acid amplification testing detects selected microbial genetic targets by amplifying or otherwise measuring pathogen-specific nucleic acid.",
            "id": "principle",
            "title": "Principle"
          },
          {
            "content": "NAAT can provide sensitive, specific, and relatively rapid evidence for an organism or resistance marker that may be difficult or slow to detect by other methods.",
            "id": "purpose",
            "title": "Purpose"
          },
          {
            "content": "Performance depends on the right source, collection quality, timing, transport, and whether the assay is validated for that specimen and clinical syndrome.",
            "id": "specimen-preparation",
            "title": "Specimen and preparation"
          },
          {
            "content": "The clinical laboratory uses a validated single-target, multiplex, or quantitative platform with internal controls. The target list must be known because a negative panel excludes only what it actually tests.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Method"
          },
          {
            "content": "A detected target supports presence of the genetic material, not automatically viable organisms or causation. Correlate with symptoms, site, burden when quantitative, and alternative diagnoses.",
            "id": "interpretation",
            "title": "Interpretation"
          },
          {
            "content": "Contamination, inhibitors, sequence variation, residual nucleic acid after treatment, and targets absent from the assay can mislead. Culture may still be needed for epidemiology or susceptibility.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use Standard Precautions for collection and preserve patient privacy because molecular results can carry sensitive diagnostic implications.",
            "id": "safety",
            "title": "Safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI diagnostic principles describe molecular testing as target-specific and highly dependent on specimen and clinical interpretation.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Nucleic acid amplification testing detects selected microbial genetic targets by amplifying or otherwise measuring pathogen-specific nucleic acid.",
