        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Infectious disease serology measures host antibodies or another defined immune response to an infectious agent rather than detecting the intact organism directly.",
            "id": "principle",
            "title": "Principle"
          },
          {
            "content": "Serology can support evidence of recent infection, past exposure, immune response, or vaccination when the assay and antibody pattern are appropriate to the clinical question.",
            "id": "purpose",
            "title": "Purpose"
          },
          {
            "content": "Timing is essential because antibodies require time to develop and may persist after infection. The requested antibody class, antigen target, and need for paired samples vary by disease.",
            "id": "specimen-preparation",
            "title": "Specimen and preparation"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use the validated screening, supplemental, confirmatory, avidity, or paired-sample algorithm required for the suspected infection; do not substitute one antibody result for the complete algorithm.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Method"
          },
          {
            "content": "Antibody class, magnitude, change over time, vaccination history, prior infection, and immune status together determine whether a result supports recent, remote, or uncertain exposure.",
            "id": "interpretation",
            "title": "Interpretation"
          },
          {
            "content": "Window periods, cross-reactivity, passive antibody, vaccination, immunosuppression, and persistent antibodies can mislead. A positive antibody result does not automatically mean active infection.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Treat specimens with Standard Precautions and communicate uncertainty clearly when confirmatory or repeat testing is needed.",
            "id": "safety",
            "title": "Safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI diagnostic principles distinguish host-response serology from direct organism detection and emphasize timing and confirmatory interpretation.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Infectious disease serology measures host antibodies or another defined immune response to an infectious agent rather than detecting the intact organism directly.",
        "type": "microbiology",
        "typedAliases": [
          {
            "identity": true,
            "kind": "common-name",
            "value": "infectious disease serology"
          }
        ]
