        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Microbiology specimen transport preserves the clinically relevant agent and specimen characteristics between collection and laboratory processing while containing infectious material safely.",
            "id": "principle",
            "title": "Principle"
          },
          {
            "content": "Correct container, medium, temperature category, timing, packaging, and documentation reduce organism loss, overgrowth, leakage, and misidentification of the collection source.",
            "id": "purpose",
            "title": "Purpose"
          },
          {
            "content": "Confirm that the specimen, container, request, source label, collection time, and transport requirements match the laboratory's test directory before dispatch.",
            "id": "specimen-preparation",
            "title": "Specimen and preparation"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use the approved leak-resistant packaging and transport pathway for the exact specimen. Follow local laboratory and occupational rules rather than improvising temperature or media requirements.",
            "id": "procedure",
            "title": "Method"
          },
          {
            "content": "A result from a delayed, leaking, mislabeled, dried, or otherwise compromised specimen may not represent the patient's infection status and should be interpreted with a documented quality limitation.",
            "id": "interpretation",
            "title": "Interpretation"
          },
          {
            "content": "No single transport condition fits bacteria, viruses, anaerobes, fungi, parasites, toxins, or molecular targets. Correct transport cannot repair collection from the wrong site.",
            "id": "limitations",
            "title": "Limitations"
          },
          {
            "content": "Contain specimens as potentially infectious and report leakage, breakage, labeling discrepancies, and exposure immediately through the approved workflow.",
            "id": "safety",
            "title": "Safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "NCBI diagnostic principles and CDC Standard Precautions connect specimen preservation with safe containment and reliable interpretation.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
