        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Actinomyces israelii is a clinically important bacterium associated with cervicofacial actinomycosis, thoracic actinomycosis, and abdominopelvic actinomycosis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Actinomyces israelii is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Actinomyces israelii is a Gram-positive filamentous with a obligate anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and extracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Dental health, mucosal and surgical care, and prompt assessment of chronic indurated lesions reduce delayed recognition; no isolation is needed. The reviewed route description is an endogenous, incompletely defined, or nonroutine acquisition pattern for which ANI does not assert a person-to-person transmission route. Endogenous disease evolves slowly after mucosal disruption and has no defined exposure date. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "An oral and gastrointestinal commensal becomes invasive after mucosal disruption, producing chronic contiguous inflammation, sinus tracts, fibrosis, and sulfur granules. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Actinomyces israelii is associated with cervicofacial actinomycosis, thoracic actinomycosis, and abdominopelvic actinomycosis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Histopathology and prolonged anaerobic culture of tissue or aspirate support diagnosis; superficial swabs often reflect colonization or miss the organism. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Dental health, mucosal and surgical care, and prompt assessment of chronic indurated lesions reduce delayed recognition; no isolation is needed. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Actinomyces, Propionibacterium propionicus, and Streptomyces. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
