        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Ancylostoma duodenale is a clinically important nematode (roundworm) associated with hookworm disease, iron-deficiency anemia, and ground itch with pulmonary migration. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ancylostoma duodenale is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the nematode class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ancylostoma duodenale is a nematode (roundworm) with a nematode clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Wear footwear, improve sanitation, avoid fecally contaminated soil, and support anemia and endemic deworming programs. The reviewed route description is percutaneous soil transmission. Skin and pulmonary findings can occur early, while intestinal egg production and anemia develop over weeks to months. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Larvae enter through skin, pass through lung, and mature in small intestine, where blood feeding can cause chronic iron loss and protein depletion. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ancylostoma duodenale is associated with hookworm disease, iron-deficiency anemia, and ground itch with pulmonary migration. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Stool ova examination confirms hookworm infection but usually cannot distinguish Ancylostoma from Necator without additional validated methods. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Wear footwear, improve sanitation, avoid fecally contaminated soil, and support anemia and endemic deworming programs. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Intestinal Hookworm. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
