        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Aspergillus fumigatus is a clinically important fungus associated with allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, aspergilloma, and invasive aspergillosis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Aspergillus fumigatus is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the fungus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Aspergillus fumigatus is a fungus with a mold clinical morphology, nonmotile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship; its reviewed oxygen profile is obligate aerobe. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Protect severely immunocompromised patients from construction dust and high-burden mold exposure according to facility policy; routine person-to-person isolation is not used. The reviewed route description is environmental inhalation. Environmental exposure is frequent and onset depends strongly on host immune state, making one exposure-to-disease interval unreliable. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Inhaled conidia are usually cleared, but allergy, damaged lung cavities, neutropenia, or major immune suppression can permit airway, cavity, or angioinvasive disease. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Aspergillus fumigatus is associated with allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, aspergilloma, and invasive aspergillosis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Diagnosis integrates host risk, imaging, culture or histopathology, and validated fungal biomarkers; colonization or one biomarker alone is not definitive. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Protect severely immunocompromised patients from construction dust and high-burden mold exposure according to facility policy; routine person-to-person isolation is not used. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC: About Aspergillosis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
