        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Bacillus anthracis is a clinically important bacterium associated with cutaneous anthrax, inhalation anthrax, gastrointestinal anthrax, and injection anthrax. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacillus anthracis is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacillus anthracis is a Gram-positive bacillus with a facultative anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, spore-forming, nonmotile, and extracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Occupational vaccination where indicated, animal-health control, exposure management, and avoidance of suspect animal products reduce risk; routine person spread is uncommon. The reviewed route description is the reviewed zoonotic transmission and environmental exposure transmission route categories. Onset varies substantially by cutaneous, inhalational, gastrointestinal, or injection route, and inhalational spores can remain dormant. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Environmentally persistent spores can germinate after route-specific exposure; capsule and edema and lethal toxins support local injury, edema, and systemic shock. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacillus anthracis is associated with cutaneous anthrax, inhalation anthrax, gastrointestinal anthrax, and injection anthrax. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Suspected anthrax requires immediate clinical, laboratory, and public-health coordination with culture and molecular confirmation from an appropriate specimen. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Occupational vaccination where indicated, animal-health control, exposure management, and avoidance of suspect animal products reduce risk; routine person spread is uncommon. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Anthrax. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
