        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Bacillus cereus is a clinically important bacterium associated with emetic food poisoning, diarrheal food poisoning, post-traumatic endophthalmitis, and device-associated bacteremia. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacillus cereus is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacillus cereus is a Gram-positive bacillus with a facultative anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, spore-forming, motile, and extracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Rapid refrigeration, safe holding temperatures, food hygiene, wound protection, and aseptic device care reduce disease. The reviewed route description is the reviewed foodborne transmission and environmental exposure transmission route categories. Preformed emetic toxin causes rapid onset, whereas diarrheal and invasive syndromes have different timing. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Heat-stable emetic toxin or enterotoxins cause distinct foodborne syndromes, while tissue-destructive factors can drive severe ocular or invasive infection. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Bacillus cereus is associated with emetic food poisoning, diarrheal food poisoning, post-traumatic endophthalmitis, and device-associated bacteremia. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Typical food poisoning is often clinical and outbreak based; culture of sterile sites or implicated food supports invasive disease or public-health investigation. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Rapid refrigeration, safe holding temperatures, food hygiene, wound protection, and aseptic device care reduce disease. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Food Safety. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
