        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Clonorchis sinensis is a clinically important trematode (fluke) associated with clonorchiasis, cholangitis or biliary obstruction, and cholangiocarcinoma risk. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clonorchis sinensis is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the trematode class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clonorchis sinensis is a trematode (fluke) with a trematode clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook freshwater fish thoroughly, avoid raw pickled or inadequately frozen fish where endemic, and improve sanitation to protect aquaculture waters. The reviewed route description is foodborne transmission. Adult maturation takes weeks, while biliary symptoms and cancer risk reflect chronic infection over years. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Metacercariae in raw freshwater fish mature in bile ducts, where chronic epithelial irritation and inflammation can cause obstruction and increase cholangiocarcinoma risk. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clonorchis sinensis is associated with clonorchiasis, cholangitis or biliary obstruction, and cholangiocarcinoma risk. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Stool egg examination plus biliary imaging and dietary geography support diagnosis; eggs can resemble other small liver flukes. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook freshwater fish thoroughly, avoid raw pickled or inadequately frozen fish where endemic, and improve sanitation to protect aquaculture waters. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Clonorchiasis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
