        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Clostridium tetani is a clinically important bacterium associated with generalized tetanus, localized tetanus, cephalic tetanus, and neonatal tetanus. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clostridium tetani is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clostridium tetani is a Gram-positive bacillus with a obligate anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, spore-forming, motile, and extracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Routine vaccination with boosters, pregnancy immunization, and guideline-based wound assessment and prophylaxis prevent disease; tetanus is not spread person to person. The reviewed route description is the reviewed environmental exposure transmission route categories. The reviewed incubation estimate is 3 to 21 days; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Spores germinate in low-oxygen damaged tissue and tetanospasmin travels along nerves to block inhibitory neurotransmitter release, causing rigidity and spasms. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Clostridium tetani is associated with generalized tetanus, localized tetanus, cephalic tetanus, and neonatal tetanus. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Tetanus is a clinical diagnosis; wound culture is neither sensitive nor specific enough to exclude or confirm disease alone. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Routine vaccination with boosters, pregnancy immunization, and guideline-based wound assessment and prophylaxis prevent disease; tetanus is not spread person to person. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Tetanus. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
