        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Ehrlichia chaffeensis is a clinically important bacterium associated with human monocytic ehrlichiosis, febrile cytopenic illness, meningoencephalitis, and multiorgan failure. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ehrlichia chaffeensis is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ehrlichia chaffeensis is a Gram-negative coccobacillus with a unknown oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and obligate intracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Tick avoidance, repellents, protective clothing, tick checks, and prompt removal reduce risk; routine patient isolation is unnecessary. The reviewed route description is the reviewed vector-borne transmission route categories. The reviewed incubation estimate is 5 to 14 days; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Tick-transmitted organisms replicate in monocyte vacuoles as morulae, dysregulating immunity and producing systemic inflammation, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and hepatitis. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ehrlichia chaffeensis is associated with human monocytic ehrlichiosis, febrile cytopenic illness, meningoencephalitis, and multiorgan failure. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Whole-blood PCR is most useful early; paired serology confirms retrospectively, and a peripheral smear is insensitive despite possible morulae. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Tick avoidance, repellents, protective clothing, tick checks, and prompt removal reduce risk; routine patient isolation is unnecessary. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Ehrlichiosis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
