        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Entamoeba histolytica is a clinically important protozoan parasite associated with amebic colitis, amebic dysentery, and amebic liver abscess. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Entamoeba histolytica is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the protozoan class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan parasite with a protozoal clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use safe water, food hygiene, sanitation, and handwashing, and reduce fecal exposure during sexual contact. The reviewed route description is fecal oral transmission. Symptoms often begin within weeks but can occur months or years after acquisition, especially with later invasive disease. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Ingested cysts release trophozoites that may remain luminal or invade colonic mucosa and, through portal spread, form liver abscesses. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Entamoeba histolytica is associated with amebic colitis, amebic dysentery, and amebic liver abscess. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Species-specific antigen or molecular testing helps distinguish pathogenic E. histolytica from nonpathogenic look-alikes; imaging and serology support extraintestinal disease. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use safe water, food hygiene, sanitation, and handwashing, and reduce fecal exposure during sexual contact. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Amebiasis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
