        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Enterococcus faecium is a clinically important bacterium associated with bloodstream infection, urinary tract infection, infective endocarditis, and intra-abdominal infection. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Enterococcus faecium is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Enterococcus faecium is a Gram-positive coccus with a facultative anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and extracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use hand hygiene, environmental cleaning, device stewardship, antimicrobial stewardship, and facility guidance for vancomycin-resistant enterococci. The reviewed route description is contact transmission. Colonization can be prolonged before endogenous or healthcare-associated infection appears. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "A gastrointestinal commensal and healthcare-adapted opportunist notable for environmental persistence, device association, and frequent multidrug resistance including vancomycin-resistant phenotypes. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Enterococcus faecium is associated with bloodstream infection, urinary tract infection, infective endocarditis, and intra-abdominal infection. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Culture, species identification, and isolate-specific susceptibility testing are essential; a resistance phenotype is not an identity alias for every E. faecium isolate. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use hand hygiene, environmental cleaning, device stewardship, antimicrobial stewardship, and facility guidance for vancomycin-resistant enterococci. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Vancomycin-resistant Enterococci. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
