        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Epstein-Barr virus is a clinically important virus associated with infectious mononucleosis, post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease, and EBV-associated malignancy. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Epstein-Barr virus is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the virus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Epstein-Barr virus is an acellular, obligately intracellular virus with a dsdna genome and a enveloped particle. Envelope status helps explain environmental stability and susceptibility to routine controls, while genome type shapes replication and diagnostic targets. Capsid symmetry is left explicitly unknown in this bounded record rather than inferred without a reviewed taxonomic source.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "No licensed vaccine is routinely available; avoiding saliva exchange during acute illness and transplant-specific monitoring reduce selected risks. The reviewed route description is body fluid transmission. The reviewed incubation estimate is 28 to 42 days; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "EBV infects oropharyngeal epithelium and B lymphocytes, establishes latency, and can drive lymphoid proliferation when immune control is impaired. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Epstein-Barr virus is associated with infectious mononucleosis, post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease, and EBV-associated malignancy. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Heterophile and EBV-specific serology support infectious mononucleosis diagnosis; nucleic-acid monitoring is reserved for selected immunocompromised contexts. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "No licensed vaccine is routinely available; avoiding saliva exchange during acute illness and transplant-specific monitoring reduce selected risks. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses: Master Species List; CDC: About Epstein-Barr Virus. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
