        "definition": "Escherichia coli is a Gram-negative, facultatively anaerobic bacillus that normally inhabits the intestines of humans and other warm-blooded animals. Most strains are harmless members of the gut microbiota, but specific pathotypes and extraintestinal strains can cause diarrhea, urinary tract infection, bloodstream infection, pneumonia, neonatal meningitis, and sepsis. The species name alone therefore does not tell whether an isolate is harmless, virulent, or clinically significant.",
