        "name": "Helicobacter pylori",
        "primaryDomain": "Microbiology",
        "relationshipNotApplicable": [],
        "searchTerms": [
          "gastric helicobacter",
          "H. pylori",
          "helicobacter organism",
          "Helicobacter pylori",
          "peptic ulcer bacterium",
          "urease positive curved rod"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Helicobacter pylori is a curved, microaerophilic Gram-negative bacterium adapted to long-term residence in the gastric mucus layer. Persistent infection causes chronic gastritis and is a major cause of peptic ulcer disease; it also raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and gastric MALT lymphoma. Infection may be silent for years, so finding the organism and deciding whether symptoms are caused by it are related but distinct clinical steps.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "H. pylori is one Helicobacter species. The abbreviation names the organism, while H. pylori infection, gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, and gastric cancer name different clinical outcomes. Not every infected person develops an ulcer, and not every ulcer is caused by H. pylori because NSAIDs and other disorders can produce similar injury.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "The organism is a curved or spiral, motile, non-spore-forming Gram-negative rod that grows best under low-oxygen conditions. Flagella let it move through gastric mucus. Urease converts urea to ammonia and carbon dioxide, buffering acid in the local microenvironment. This does not neutralize the entire stomach; it creates a narrow protective niche that helps the organism survive close to epithelial cells.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Humans are the principal reservoir, with colonization centered in gastric mucus and along the epithelial surface. Transmission is incompletely defined but can involve contact with infected saliva, vomit, or fecal material, often acquired in childhood. Once established, infection can persist for decades without eradication therapy, so a short species-wide incubation period does not describe the chronic biology.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Urease, motility, adhesins, and strain-specific factors support colonization despite acidity. Chronic immune activation injures the mucosa and alters acid regulation. Antral-predominant inflammation can increase gastrin and acid delivery to the duodenum, favoring duodenal ulceration, whereas extensive gastric injury can impair protective mechanisms and contribute to gastric ulcers and atrophy. Repeated epithelial injury and repair help explain the long-term cancer association.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Many infected people have no symptoms. When disease occurs, dyspepsia or ulcer pain may be present, but symptoms alone cannot establish the organism. Gastrointestinal bleeding can produce melena, hematemesis, anemia, dizziness, or shock; sudden severe abdominal pain can signal perforation. These are emergency findings, not indications to wait for routine outpatient testing.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Urea breath testing and stool antigen testing detect active infection and can also confirm eradication when timed correctly after therapy. Endoscopy with biopsy supports diagnosis when alarm features or another indication exists. Serology may reflect prior exposure and cannot reliably prove current infection or cure. Proton-pump inhibitors, bismuth, and recent antibiotics can suppress organism burden and create false-negative active tests, so preparation and timing matter.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Hand hygiene, safe food and water practices, and avoidance of exposure to vomit or fecal contamination reduce enteric transmission opportunities. Eradication requires a complete combination regimen selected for prior antibiotic exposure, local resistance, allergy, and current guidance, followed by a test of cure. One antibiotic alone is not adequate and encourages failure or resistance. Bleeding, perforation, obstruction, or suspected cancer requires urgent disease-focused care.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed against NCBI Taxonomy and NIH/NIDDK peptic-ulcer causes, diagnostic, and treatment guidance. The amoxicillin edge represents one possible component of an appropriate combination regimen, never monotherapy.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Helicobacter pylori is a curved, microaerophilic Gram-negative bacterium adapted to long-term residence in the gastric mucus layer. Persistent infection causes chronic gastritis and is a major cause of peptic ulcer disease; it also raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and gastric MALT lymphoma. Infection may be silent for years, so finding the organism and deciding whether symptoms are caused by it are related but distinct clinical steps.",
