        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Herpes simplex virus type 2 is a clinically important virus associated with genital herpes, neonatal herpes, and aseptic meningitis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Herpes simplex virus type 2 is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the virus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Herpes simplex virus type 2 is an acellular, obligately intracellular virus with a dsdna genome and a enveloped particle. Envelope status helps explain environmental stability and susceptibility to routine controls, while genome type shapes replication and diagnostic targets. Capsid symmetry is left explicitly unknown in this bounded record rather than inferred without a reviewed taxonomic source.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Barrier methods, avoiding sexual contact during symptoms, pregnancy-specific management, and patient-specific suppressive therapy guidance reduce transmission. The reviewed route description is contact transmission. Primary infection is frequently unrecognized, and recurrent shedding reflects latency rather than a new incubation period. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "HSV-2 infects mucocutaneous sites, establishes lifelong sensory-ganglion latency, and may reactivate with or without visible lesions. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Herpes simplex virus type 2 is associated with genital herpes, neonatal herpes, and aseptic meningitis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Nucleic-acid testing of an active lesion is preferred; type-specific serology can support selected evaluations but does not prove the anatomic site. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Barrier methods, avoiding sexual contact during symptoms, pregnancy-specific management, and patient-specific suppressive therapy guidance reduce transmission. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses: Master Species List; CDC: About Genital Herpes. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
