        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Human immunodeficiency virus type 2 is a clinically important virus associated with acute retroviral syndrome, chronic HIV-2 infection, and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Human immunodeficiency virus type 2 is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the virus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Human immunodeficiency virus type 2 is an acellular, obligately intracellular virus with a ssrna reverse transcribing genome and a enveloped particle. Envelope status helps explain environmental stability and susceptibility to routine controls, while genome type shapes replication and diagnostic targets. Capsid symmetry is left explicitly unknown in this bounded record rather than inferred without a reviewed taxonomic source.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Barrier methods, screened blood, sterile injection equipment, testing, and current HIV prevention and treatment guidance reduce transmission. The reviewed route description is body fluid transmission. Acute infection may be asymptomatic and time to immunodeficiency varies widely, often over years. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "HIV-2 infects CD4 T cells and establishes chronic infection, generally with lower viral loads and slower progression than HIV-1 but with the same potential for AIDS. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Human immunodeficiency virus type 2 is associated with acute retroviral syndrome, chronic HIV-2 infection, and acquired immunodeficiency syndrome. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "An HIV-1/HIV-2 differentiation algorithm and type-specific nucleic-acid testing are needed because some standard viral-load assays do not reliably quantify HIV-2. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Barrier methods, screened blood, sterile injection equipment, testing, and current HIV prevention and treatment guidance reduce transmission. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses: Master Species List; NIH ClinicalInfo: HIV-2 Infection. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
