        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Influenza B virus is an enveloped, segmented negative-sense RNA virus that causes seasonal influenza. It is a distinct infectious agent from influenza A virus, although either virus can produce clinically similar influenza illness. Influenza B circulates mainly among people and contributes substantially to seasonal outbreaks, including illness in children and older adults.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Influenza B virus belongs to the orthomyxovirus group and is classified separately from influenza A virus. Its surface proteins include hemagglutinin and neuraminidase, but influenza B viruses are not divided into the H-and-N subtype system used for influenza A. Antigenic drift can change circulating influenza B viruses over time, which is one reason seasonal surveillance and vaccine review remain important.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "The virus has a lipid envelope and eight negative-sense RNA genome segments packaged in helical ribonucleoprotein complexes. Hemagglutinin supports attachment and entry into susceptible respiratory epithelial cells, while neuraminidase supports release of newly formed virions. Replication requires a living host cell, and the lipid envelope is vulnerable to detergents and appropriately used disinfectants.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "People with influenza B spread virus mainly through respiratory particles during close contact. Transmission can begin before symptoms are recognized, and contaminated hands may contribute when virus reaches the eyes, nose, or mouth. Crowded indoor settings and close household or healthcare contact can increase exposure opportunities, so symptom screening alone cannot prevent every transmission event.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Influenza B virus infects respiratory epithelial cells, where replication injures the epithelium and triggers innate inflammatory responses that contribute to fever, myalgia, fatigue, and cough. Epithelial injury can impair mucociliary clearance and increase vulnerability to viral pneumonia or secondary bacterial pneumonia. Renewed fever, hypoxemia, respiratory decline, or worsening after initial improvement warrants prompt reassessment.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Influenza B commonly causes abrupt fever, cough, headache, myalgia, malaise, and sore throat, but age and immune status can alter the presentation. Important complications include pneumonia, dehydration, encephalopathy, myositis, and exacerbation of chronic cardiopulmonary disease. Nursing assessment should escalate respiratory distress, cyanosis, chest pain, confusion, severe weakness, poor oral intake, or clinical deterioration after initial improvement.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Molecular assays such as RT-PCR can distinguish influenza B from influenza A and are preferred for many hospitalized or high-risk patients. Rapid antigen tests are less sensitive, so a negative rapid result does not exclude influenza when clinical suspicion remains high. Interpretation depends on specimen quality, collection timing, local circulation, and pretest probability; treatment decisions for priority patients should not be delayed solely while awaiting a result.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Seasonal influenza vaccination includes reviewed influenza B components and reduces the risk of influenza and severe outcomes, although formulations are updated as circulating viruses change. Standard Precautions plus current syndrome-appropriate respiratory precautions, hand hygiene, source control, ventilation, and avoiding close contact while acutely ill reduce spread. Antiviral selection and timing require patient-specific clinical assessment and current guidance rather than organism identity alone.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Draft evidence basis: NCBI Taxonomy for stable identity and CDC influenza clinical, Pink Book, virus-change, testing, antiviral, vaccination, and isolation-precaution guidance. This review-only entry does not replace current institutional infection-control policy or patient-specific clinical judgment.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
