        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus is a clinically important virus associated with Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the virus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus is an acellular, obligately intracellular virus with a dsdna genome and a enveloped particle. Envelope status helps explain environmental stability and susceptibility to routine controls, while genome type shapes replication and diagnostic targets. Capsid symmetry is left explicitly unknown in this bounded record rather than inferred without a reviewed taxonomic source.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "No licensed vaccine is available; safer sexual practices, blood safety, and effective management of immunosuppression reduce selected risks. The reviewed route description is body fluid transmission. Acquisition is often silent and the interval to KSHV-associated malignancy depends strongly on immune status and can span years. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "KSHV establishes latent infection and expresses oncogenic and immune-modulating proteins that can promote vascular or lymphoid tumors when host control is impaired. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Kaposi sarcoma-associated herpesvirus is associated with Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, and multicentric Castleman disease. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Diagnosis of KSHV-associated disease relies on compatible clinical findings plus tissue pathology and virus-specific markers rather than screening PCR alone. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "No licensed vaccine is available; safer sexual practices, blood safety, and effective management of immunosuppression reduce selected risks. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses: Master Species List; NIH National Cancer Institute: Infectious Agents and Cancer. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
