        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Leishmania donovani is a clinically important protozoan parasite associated with visceral leishmaniasis, fever with splenomegaly, and pancytopenic disseminated infection. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Leishmania donovani is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the protozoan class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Leishmania donovani is a protozoan parasite with a protozoal clinical morphology, variable motility classification, and obligate intracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Prevent sand-fly bites with protective clothing, screened sleeping areas, and vector measures; promptly evaluate prolonged fever after endemic exposure. The reviewed route description is vector borne transmission. Visceral disease may appear within weeks or remain clinically silent for months to years depending on host and exposure. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Sand-fly inoculation leads to macrophage infection; visceral spread to spleen, liver, and marrow causes prolonged fever, organ enlargement, and cytopenias. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Leishmania donovani is associated with visceral leishmaniasis, fever with splenomegaly, and pancytopenic disseminated infection. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Tissue parasite detection, molecular testing, and region-appropriate serology are paired with blood counts and organ assessment. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Prevent sand-fly bites with protective clothing, screened sleeping areas, and vector measures; promptly evaluate prolonged fever after endemic exposure. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Leishmaniasis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
