        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Mycobacterium leprae is a clinically important bacterium associated with paucibacillary Hansen disease, multibacillary Hansen disease, peripheral neuropathy, and leprosy reactions. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Mycobacterium leprae is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Mycobacterium leprae is a Gram-reaction indeterminate bacillus with a obligate aerobe oxygen profile. It is acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and obligate intracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Early diagnosis and effective multidrug therapy interrupt transmission; evaluate close contacts while avoiding stigma because brief casual contact is low risk. The reviewed route description is contact transmission. The incubation period is usually measured in years and varies widely with exposure and host immunity. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "A slow-growing intracellular bacillus targets macrophages and Schwann cells in cooler tissues, producing chronic skin lesions, sensory loss, and nerve injury shaped by host immunity. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Mycobacterium leprae is associated with paucibacillary Hansen disease, multibacillary Hansen disease, peripheral neuropathy, and leprosy reactions. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Diagnosis combines skin and neurologic examination with biopsy and acid-fast assessment; the organism is not grown on routine artificial media. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Early diagnosis and effective multidrug therapy interrupt transmission; evaluate close contacts while avoiding stigma because brief casual contact is low risk. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Hansen's Disease. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
