        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Nocardia asteroides complex is a clinically important bacterium associated with pulmonary nocardiosis, disseminated nocardiosis, brain abscess, and primary cutaneous nocardiosis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Nocardia asteroides complex is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Nocardia asteroides complex is a Gram-positive filamentous with a obligate aerobe oxygen profile. It is partially acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and facultative intracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "There is no routine person-to-person spread; immunocompromised patients should use prudent soil and dust exposure precautions and promptly assess pulmonary or neurologic symptoms. The reviewed route description is the reviewed environmental exposure transmission route categories. Environmental exposure is usually not identified and pulmonary or disseminated disease may evolve over weeks to months. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Environmental branching bacteria resist oxidative killing and can persist in phagocytes, with dissemination from lung to brain favored by impaired cellular immunity. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Nocardia asteroides complex is associated with pulmonary nocardiosis, disseminated nocardiosis, brain abscess, and primary cutaneous nocardiosis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Microscopy, modified acid-fast staining, culture, species identification, and susceptibility testing use a respiratory, tissue, or sterile-site specimen with laboratory notification. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "There is no routine person-to-person spread; immunocompromised patients should use prudent soil and dust exposure precautions and promptly assess pulmonary or neurologic symptoms. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; NCBI Bookshelf: Nocardia. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
