        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Plasmodium knowlesi is a clinically important protozoan parasite associated with knowlesi malaria, severe malaria, and malarial thrombocytopenia. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Plasmodium knowlesi is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the protozoan class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Plasmodium knowlesi is a protozoan parasite with a protozoal clinical morphology, variable motility classification, and obligate intracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Prevent mosquito bites in forest-edge areas and urgently evaluate fever after travel because severe disease can develop despite initially nonspecific findings. The reviewed route description is zoonotic vector borne transmission. The reviewed incubation estimate is 9 to 12 days; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "A macaque-associated malaria parasite transmitted by mosquitoes completes a rapid red-cell cycle, allowing parasite burden and organ dysfunction to rise quickly. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Plasmodium knowlesi is associated with knowlesi malaria, severe malaria, and malarial thrombocytopenia. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Microscopy may resemble other Plasmodium species, so travel geography and validated molecular confirmation are important when knowlesi malaria is possible. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Prevent mosquito bites in forest-edge areas and urgently evaluate fever after travel because severe disease can develop despite initially nonspecific findings. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Malaria. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
