        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Pthirus pubis is a clinically important ectoparasite associated with pubic pediculosis, genital pruritus, and eyelash infestation. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pthirus pubis is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the ectoparasite class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pthirus pubis is a ectoparasite with a arthropod clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Treat recent partners and household contacts as directed, avoid sexual contact until treatment is complete, and launder recently used clothing and bedding. The reviewed route description is intimate contact transmission. Itching may be delayed after first infestation and occur sooner with repeat exposure; asymptomatic transmission is possible. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pubic lice attach to coarse hair and feed on blood, producing itching and excoriations; spread is usually through close intimate contact. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pthirus pubis is associated with pubic pediculosis, genital pruritus, and eyelash infestation. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Visualization of live lice or viable nits on coarse hair confirms infestation and should prompt assessment for other sexually transmitted infections as appropriate. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Treat recent partners and household contacts as directed, avoid sexual contact until treatment is complete, and launder recently used clothing and bedding. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC: About Pubic Lice. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
