        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Sporothrix schenckii is a clinically important fungus associated with lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis, pulmonary sporotrichosis, and disseminated sporotrichosis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Sporothrix schenckii is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the fungus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Sporothrix schenckii is a fungus with a dimorphic fungus clinical morphology, nonmotile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship; its reviewed oxygen profile is unknown. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Wear gloves and protective clothing when handling thorny plants, sphagnum, soil, or animals with suspicious lesions and use wound hygiene after puncture. The reviewed route description is traumatic environmental or zoonotic inoculation. The reviewed incubation estimate is 1 to 12 weeks; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Traumatic inoculation from plant material or infected animals can produce nodules tracking along lymphatics; inhaled or disseminated disease is less common. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Sporothrix schenckii is associated with lymphocutaneous sporotrichosis, pulmonary sporotrichosis, and disseminated sporotrichosis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Culture or tissue evaluation supports diagnosis, while exposure history and the characteristic nodular lymphatic pattern guide suspicion. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Wear gloves and protective clothing when handling thorny plants, sphagnum, soil, or animals with suspicious lesions and use wound hygiene after puncture. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC: About Sporotrichosis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
