        "studentFacing": true,
        "summary": "Staphylococcus aureus is a Gram-positive coccus that commonly lives on human skin and in the nose without causing disease, yet can become an aggressive pathogen when it enters damaged skin, deeper tissue, the bloodstream, lungs, bone, joints, or implanted devices. Colonization is therefore not the same as infection: the organism may be present without tissue injury, while infection means it is multiplying in a site and provoking local or systemic harm.",
