        "definition": "Staphylococcus epidermidis is a Gram-positive coccus that commonly colonizes human skin and mucous membranes. It is usually a commensal organism, but it can cause clinically important infection when a skin barrier is breached or when it adheres to an indwelling or prosthetic device. Colonization, blood-culture contamination, and true infection are different states and must be distinguished with the specimen context and the patient's clinical findings.",
