        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Staphylococcus saprophyticus is a clinically important bacterium associated with acute uncomplicated cystitis and pyelonephritis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Staphylococcus saprophyticus is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Staphylococcus saprophyticus is a Gram-positive coccus with a facultative anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and extracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use appropriate urine collection, routine hygiene, and urinary-device stewardship; it is not managed as a sexually transmitted infection. The reviewed route description is an endogenous, incompletely defined, or nonroutine acquisition pattern for which ANI does not assert a person-to-person transmission route. Endogenous colonization may precede ascending urinary infection, so no single exposure-to-disease interval applies. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "A coagulase-negative skin and genitourinary commensal whose uroepithelial adherence and urease activity support ascending urinary infection. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Staphylococcus saprophyticus is associated with acute uncomplicated cystitis and pyelonephritis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Interpret a properly collected urine culture with symptoms and urinalysis; species identification separates it from other coagulase-negative staphylococci. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Use appropriate urine collection, routine hygiene, and urinary-device stewardship; it is not managed as a sexually transmitted infection. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC Emerging Infectious Diseases: Foodborne Origin and Spread of Staphylococcus saprophyticus Causing Urinary Tract Infections. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
