        "relationshipNotApplicable": [],
        "searchTerms": [
          "gram positive diplococci",
          "lancet shaped diplococci",
          "pneumococcal bacteria",
          "pneumococcal organism",
          "Pneumococcus",
          "S. pneumoniae",
          "Streptococcus pneumoniae"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Streptococcus pneumoniae, or pneumococcus, is an encapsulated Gram-positive coccus that commonly colonizes the human nasopharynx and can cause pneumonia, otitis media, sinusitis, bacteremia, and meningitis. Carriage is common and does not itself prove disease; clinical infection begins when the organism reaches a normally protected site or overwhelms local and systemic defenses.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "S. pneumoniae is a distinct Streptococcus species with many capsular serotypes. Pneumococcal disease names infections caused by this organism, while a pneumococcal vaccine targets selected capsular serotypes and is not an identity alias for the bacterium. The serotype matters epidemiologically and for vaccine coverage, but routine initial care still depends on syndrome, specimen quality, and susceptibility.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cells are usually lancet-shaped Gram-positive diplococci, although short chains can occur. The organism is nonmotile, non-spore-forming, and facultatively anaerobic. Its polysaccharide capsule impairs phagocytosis and is the major virulence determinant. The capsule explains both the heightened danger in asplenia, where clearance of encapsulated organisms is impaired, and the rationale for capsule-directed vaccines.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Humans carry pneumococci in the upper respiratory tract, especially children. Respiratory droplets and close direct contact move organisms between people. Carriage can persist without symptoms, then precede infection when viral inflammation, impaired mucociliary clearance, aspiration, age, immune dysfunction, or an anatomic defect permits spread into the middle ear, sinuses, lungs, bloodstream, or meninges.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "After attachment to respiratory mucosa, the capsule limits opsonization and phagocytic clearance. Local multiplication and inflammatory recruitment fill alveoli with fluid and cells during pneumonia, reducing ventilation and gas exchange. Entry into blood can produce bacteremia and seed the meninges. Because inflammation contributes much of the tissue injury, a patient may deteriorate rapidly even though the organism is extracellular.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Pneumococcal pneumonia commonly produces fever, cough, pleuritic discomfort, dyspnea, and focal findings; infants and older adults may present less typically. Invasive disease includes bacteremia and meningitis and can progress to sepsis. Risk is higher at age extremes and with asplenia, immunocompromise, cochlear implants, cerebrospinal-fluid leaks, and chronic cardiopulmonary disease. Hypotension, respiratory distress, altered mental status, or meningeal signs requires urgent escalation.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "A Gram stain of a high-quality specimen may show Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci, but morphology is a clue rather than confirmation. Culture from a normally sterile site establishes invasive disease and permits susceptibility testing. Respiratory cultures require clinical correlation because upper-airway carriage can contaminate specimens. Urinary antigen and molecular methods can support selected diagnoses but do not replace syndrome-specific interpretation.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Vaccination reduces disease from included serotypes, but it does not cover every pneumococcal strain and does not make compatible severe illness safe to ignore. Standard Precautions apply routinely. Respiratory hygiene, hand hygiene, appropriate vaccination, and management of aspiration or device risks reduce harm. Antibiotic choice must reflect infection site, severity, age, allergy, local resistance, and susceptibility rather than an assumption that every isolate remains penicillin susceptible.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed against NCBI Taxonomy and current CDC pneumococcal clinical and vaccine-prevention guidance. The ceftriaxone relationship is syndrome- and susceptibility-bound, not a universal treatment instruction.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
