        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Taenia solium is a clinically important cestode (tapeworm) associated with intestinal taeniasis, cysticercosis, and neurocysticercosis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Taenia solium is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the cestode class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Taenia solium is a cestode (tapeworm) with a cestode clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and extracellular host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook pork safely, use sanitation and hand hygiene, identify and treat tapeworm carriers, and prevent food handling by untreated carriers. The reviewed route description is foodborne and fecal oral transmission. Intestinal adult infection becomes patent over weeks, while cysticercosis symptoms can emerge months to years after egg ingestion. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Eating larval cysts in pork causes an adult intestinal tapeworm, while ingesting human-shed eggs causes tissue larvae and potentially CNS cysticercosis. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Taenia solium is associated with intestinal taeniasis, cysticercosis, and neurocysticercosis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Stool testing evaluates taeniasis; neuroimaging, serology, eye examination, and specialist assessment evaluate cysticercosis before therapy. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook pork safely, use sanitation and hand hygiene, identify and treat tapeworm carriers, and prevent food handling by untreated carriers. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Taeniasis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
