        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Trichinella spiralis is a clinically important nematode (roundworm) associated with trichinellosis, myositis with eosinophilia, and cardiac or neurologic trichinellosis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Trichinella spiralis is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the nematode class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Trichinella spiralis is a nematode (roundworm) with a nematode clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and mixed host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook meat to recommended safe temperatures, follow validated game-meat handling guidance, and do not rely on smoking, drying, or casual freezing. The reviewed route description is foodborne transmission. Gastrointestinal symptoms may begin within days, while systemic muscle disease commonly appears one to several weeks later. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Larvae from undercooked meat mature briefly in intestine, then newborn larvae disseminate and encyst within skeletal-muscle cells, driving fever, edema, and myalgia. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Trichinella spiralis is associated with trichinellosis, myositis with eosinophilia, and cardiac or neurologic trichinellosis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Food history, eosinophilia, muscle enzymes, serology over time, and occasionally tissue evidence support diagnosis; early serology can be negative. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook meat to recommended safe temperatures, follow validated game-meat handling guidance, and do not rely on smoking, drying, or casual freezing. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: Trichinellosis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
