        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Trypanosoma cruzi is a clinically important protozoan parasite associated with acute Chagas disease, Chagas cardiomyopathy, and Chagas gastrointestinal disease. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Trypanosoma cruzi is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the protozoan class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Trypanosoma cruzi is a protozoan parasite with a protozoal clinical morphology, motile motility classification, and mixed host-cell relationship. Life stage, specimen source, and host context may change what is visible, so morphology is a clue rather than a stand-alone diagnosis.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reduce triatomine exposure, screen blood and organ donations and at-risk pregnancies under current policy, and use food hygiene in endemic settings. The reviewed route description is vector borne and congenital bloodborne transmission. Timing differs for vector, oral, transfusion, transplant, and congenital routes, and chronic organ disease may appear decades later. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Vector-associated contamination or other transmission routes seed bloodstream infection; intracellular amastigotes persist in tissue and can later injure myocardium and enteric nerves. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Trypanosoma cruzi is associated with acute Chagas disease, Chagas cardiomyopathy, and Chagas gastrointestinal disease. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Acute disease uses direct or molecular parasite detection, while chronic infection generally requires concordant serologic testing and organ assessment. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reduce triatomine exposure, screen blood and organ donations and at-risk pregnancies under current policy, and use food hygiene in endemic settings. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from CDC DPDx: American Trypanosomiasis. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
