        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Varicella-zoster virus is a clinically important virus associated with varicella, herpes zoster, postherpetic neuralgia, and disseminated zoster. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Varicella-zoster virus is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the virus class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Varicella-zoster virus is an acellular, obligately intracellular virus with a dsdna genome and a enveloped particle. Envelope status helps explain environmental stability and susceptibility to routine controls, while genome type shapes replication and diagnostic targets. Capsid symmetry is left explicitly unknown in this bounded record rather than inferred without a reviewed taxonomic source.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Varicella and zoster vaccination, airborne and contact controls when indicated, and exposure management under current guidance reduce disease and spread. The reviewed route description is airborne transmission. The reviewed incubation estimate is 10 to 21 days; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Primary infection causes varicella, after which virus remains latent in sensory ganglia and may reactivate later as herpes zoster. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Varicella-zoster virus is associated with varicella, herpes zoster, postherpetic neuralgia, and disseminated zoster. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Typical disease is often clinical; lesion nucleic-acid testing is the preferred confirmation when presentation or public-health implications are uncertain. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Varicella and zoster vaccination, airborne and contact controls when indicated, and exposure management under current guidance reduce disease and spread. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from International Committee on Taxonomy of Viruses: Master Species List; CDC: About Chickenpox; CDC: About Shingles. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
