        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Yersinia enterocolitica is a clinically important bacterium associated with enterocolitis, terminal ileitis and pseudoappendicitis, bacteremia, and reactive arthritis. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Yersinia enterocolitica is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Yersinia enterocolitica is a Gram-negative bacillus with a facultative anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, not known to form spores, motile, and facultative intracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook pork thoroughly, avoid unpasteurized milk, prevent kitchen cross-contamination, and use safe water and hand hygiene. The reviewed route description is fecal oral transmission. The reviewed incubation estimate is 4 to 7 days; individual timing can still vary with inoculum, route, host factors, and the syndrome being measured. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Intestinal invasion through lymphoid tissue and Yersinia outer proteins produce enteritis and mesenteric lymphadenitis, with invasive disease favored by iron overload or immunocompromise. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Yersinia enterocolitica is associated with enterocolitis, terminal ileitis and pseudoappendicitis, bacteremia, and reactive arthritis. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Stool culture or molecular testing supports enteric disease; inform the laboratory when Yersinia is suspected and culture blood for invasive illness. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Cook pork thoroughly, avoid unpasteurized milk, prevent kitchen cross-contamination, and use safe water and hand hygiene. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Yersinia Infection. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
