        "sections": [
          {
            "content": "Yersinia pestis is a clinically important bacterium associated with bubonic plague, septicemic plague, and pneumonic plague. The organism or agent name identifies the cause or association; it is not itself the disease diagnosis, and detection must be interpreted with the specimen, host, and syndrome.",
            "id": "definition",
            "title": "Definition"
          },
          {
            "content": "Yersinia pestis is ANI's canonical clinically recognized agent name in the bacterium class. Searchable abbreviations and former or common names are navigation aids, while disease names, resistance phenotypes, and strain labels remain separate concepts. Exact external taxonomic rank is recorded as reviewed-unranked here rather than fabricated from an identity-only coverage registry.",
            "id": "identity-classification",
            "title": "Identity and classification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Yersinia pestis is a Gram-negative coccobacillus with a facultative anaerobe oxygen profile. It is not acid-fast, not known to form spores, nonmotile, and facultative intracellular in its reviewed clinical behavior. These traits help narrow an early laboratory differential, but no trait pattern alone proves identity or susceptibility.",
            "id": "structure-traits",
            "title": "Structure and traits"
          },
          {
            "content": "Rodent and flea control, avoidance of sick wildlife, respiratory protection, Droplet Precautions for pneumonic plague, and contact management reduce transmission. The reviewed route description is the reviewed vector-borne transmission and zoonotic transmission and respiratory particle transmission route categories. Bubonic and primary pneumonic plague have different routes and onset intervals. Transmission risk therefore depends on the actual route, exposure, host, setting, and stage rather than the organism name alone.",
            "id": "ecology-transmission",
            "title": "Ecology and transmission"
          },
          {
            "content": "Flea-associated inoculation or respiratory acquisition is followed by lymphatic or pulmonary growth, antiphagocytic type III secretion, and possible bloodstream dissemination. This mechanism explains the connection between exposure, host vulnerability, tissue effects, and the major syndromes instead of presenting the association as an isolated fact.",
            "id": "pathogenesis",
            "title": "Pathogenesis"
          },
          {
            "content": "Yersinia pestis is associated with bubonic plague, septicemic plague, and pneumonic plague. Severity and presentation vary by age, immune status, pregnancy, anatomic site, inoculum, and comorbidity. Urgent escalation is based on the syndrome—such as shock, respiratory compromise, rapidly progressive neurologic findings, severe dehydration, or invasive infection—not merely on a positive organism label.",
            "id": "clinical-significance",
            "title": "Clinical significance"
          },
          {
            "content": "Notify the laboratory and public-health authorities when plague is suspected; culture, molecular testing, and serology use syndrome-appropriate specimens. A positive result can represent infection, colonization, past exposure, residual material, or contamination depending on the method and source; a negative result can reflect timing, specimen quality, prior therapy, or an untested target.",
            "id": "diagnostic-identification",
            "title": "Diagnostic identification"
          },
          {
            "content": "Rodent and flea control, avoidance of sick wildlife, respiratory protection, Droplet Precautions for pneumonic plague, and contact management reduce transmission. Standard Precautions remain the healthcare baseline. Add transmission-based, public-health, pregnancy, vector, food, water, or occupational measures only when current organism- and setting-specific guidance supports them.",
            "id": "prevention-safety",
            "title": "Prevention and safety"
          },
          {
            "content": "Reviewed from NCBI Taxonomy Browser; NCBI Bookshelf Medical Microbiology: Bacteriology; CDC: About Plague. Relationships are exact-ID or exact-title links; treatment and resistance are deliberately omitted when the bounded evidence cannot support a safe context-specific assertion.",
            "id": "sources",
            "title": "Sources"
          }
        ],
